{"type":"message","id":"34f70865","parentId":"0a894af9","timestamp":"2026-06-04T11:56:18.810Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the full picture clearly. Let me trace the DOT trades today:\n\n1. **01:06:56** - SELL 183.2 @ 1.092 → opened DOT SHORT ✓ ($200)\n2. **01:33:31** - BUY 194.9 @ 1.105 → closed part of SHORT + opened LONG? This net position would be complex.\n3. **09:05:25** - SELL 11.7 @ 1.099 → closed the LONG residue (our manual close) ✓\n4. **10:05:28** - SELL 195.9 @ 1.007 → opened DOT SHORT ✓ ($197)\n5. **10:07:08** - BUY 207.6 @ 1.036 → closed SHORT + could have flipped to LONG\n6. **11:41:40** - SELL 193.1 @ 1.080 → our close attempt (降级平仓) ✓\n7. **13:35:43** - BUY 0.8 + 5.6 + 175.0 = 181.4 @ 1.057 → closed the DOT SHORT (SL)\n8. **15:24:32** - SELL 191.0 @ 1.046 → opened DOT SHORT ✓ ($200)\n9. **17:42:39** - BUY 202.7 @ 1.017 → this should close the SHORT of 191.0 and flip to LONG of 11.7\n\nSo at 17:42:39, a BUY of 202.7 DOT @ 1.017 happened. The SHORT was 191.0, so:\n- BUY 202.7 closes 191.0 SHORT (net: +11.7 LONG)\n- Entry price for the new LONG: 1.017\n\nThat's exactly what we see! The SL Algo triggered a BUY of 202.7 DOT, which was MORE than the 191.0 SHORT, so it flipped to a LONG of 11.7 DOT.\n\nBut WHY was the Algo order for 202.7 DOT instead of 191.0? The Algo should be set to close the exact position amount.\n\nLet me check: the SL Algo was updated multiple times. When the Algo order was submitted, what quantity was used?\n\nLooking at the log:\n```\n15:24:31 📌 DOTUSDT 止损 Algo 单已提交：1.07 algoId=1000000095701730\n```\n\nThen it was updated several times:\n```\n17:21:07 📌 DOT 止损 Algo 单已更新：1.0679 → 1.0342\n17:22:34 📌 DOT 止损 Algo 单已更新：1.0342 → 1.0332\n...\n17:23:50 📌 DOT 止损 Algo 单已更新：1.0322 → 1.0312\n```\n\nEach update resubmits the Algo order. The question is: what quantity was used for these updates?\n\nIf the Algo update used the LOCAL CACHE quantity (which was 191.0 at first, then later corrected by 反转重建 to 196.7), and the order was submitted with 196.7 instead of 191.0 (the actual position size), then the SL trigger would:\n- BUY 196.7 DOT\n- Close 191.0 SHORT\n- Leave 5.7 LONG\n\nBut the trade shows 202.7, not 196.7. And the residual is 11.7, not 5.7.\n\nHmm, 202.7 vs 196.7 is close (202.7/196.7 = 1.03, ~3% difference). And 202.7 - 191.0 = 11.7. That matches exactly!\n\nBut wait, why would the Algo be for 202.7? Let me think...\n\nIf the Algo was updated using a \"new\" quantity that was calculated as:\n- format_quantity(200 / current_price) where current_price was around 0.99 or so\n- 200 / 0.99 = 202.0 → that's close to 202.7\n\nOr the local cache at the time of Algo update had amount=196.7 (from 反转重建). But the Algo order quantity was 202.7.\n\nActually, let me check the submit_initial_algo_orders function to see what quantity it uses.\n\nWait, there's another possibility. The monitor might be SUBMITTING a NEW Algo order with the position amount from the local cache (196.7) instead of CANCELLING the old one first. If the old Algo was still active AND the new one was submitted, there could be TWO Algo orders executing:\n1. Old Algo: close 191.0 (from initial submission)\n2. New Algo: close 196.7 (from update with corrected local cache)\n\nBut 11.7 + 191.0 ≠ 202.7. So that doesn't explain the 202.7 buy.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. What if:\n- The old SL Algo triggered (close 191.0 SHORT)\n- This executed as BUY 191.0, leaving zero position\n- Then immediately after, the monitor detected the position was closed\n- The monitor submitted a NEW open LONG order\n- But this LONG was opened with 11.7 DOT somehow\n\nLet me check: after the SL trigger at 17:42:39, the monitor would detect the position was gone. Then in the next cycle, if DOT scores triggered, the monitor would open a new position.\n\nBut the trade history only shows one trade at 17:42:39: BUY 202.7 @ 1.017. No LONG open trade after that.\n\nSo the BUY 202.7 ITSELF created the 11.7 LONG. The Algo order was set to buy 202.7 DOT, which was more than the 191.0 SHORT, flipping it to LONG 11.7.\n\nThe question remains: why 202.7? Where did this number come from?\n\nLet me check the Algo order update code. What quantity is used when updating an Algo order?","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_mKNQ9rEzgbzPQqggZ4WC5669","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"Algo.*更新\\|algo.*update\\|更新.*止损\\|update.*algo\\|_update_algo\\|sync_stop_loss\\|submit_initial_algo\" /root/.openclaw/workspace/scripts/crypto_signal_monitor.py 2>/dev/null | head -15","timeout":5}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":95758,"output":2826,"cacheRead":19968,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":118552,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1780574139801}}
