{"type":"message","id":"f5697431","parentId":"c62b34ed","timestamp":"2026-06-04T11:57:40.462Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Only 2 trades:\n1. 15:24:32 - SELL 191.0 @ 1.046 → opens DOT SHORT 191.0 (correct)\n2. 17:42:39 - BUY 202.7 @ 1.017 → closes 191.0 SHORT + opens 11.7 LONG\n\nThe BUY of 202.7 DOT closed the 191.0 SHORT and left 11.7 LONG.\n\nIf the Algo order used `closePosition=true`, it should close exactly the position size (191.0). But it bought 202.7 instead.\n\nWait, maybe `closePosition=true` on a Algo CONDITIONAL order means something different. Let me check the Binance docs.\n\nActually, on Binance Futures, `closePosition=true` auto-calculates the quantity based on the current position. For a SHORT position, it will place a BUY order with the exact position amount.\n\nBut the BUY was 202.7 not 191.0. This could only mean the position at the moment of trigger was 202.7 DOT SHORT, not 191.0.\n\nHow could the position grow from 191.0 to 202.7? UNLESS:\n- The monitor submitted an Algo update with a NEW stop order, and\n- The FUNDING from the unrealized PnL somehow added to the position\n- OR the monitor opened a parallel position\n\nActually, Binance futures positions don't grow on their own in ISOLATED margin mode. The positionAmt only changes through trades.\n\nWait, but the Algo update is not just a price update. Let me look at Algo update implementation again. When the monitor \"updates\" the SL, it does:\n1. Cancel the old Algo STOP_MARKET order\n2. Submit a new Algo STOP_MARKET order\n\nBut the new Algo order has `closePosition=true`. Could submitting a new Algo with `closePosition=true` somehow affect an existing position?\n\nNo, Algo orders don't affect positions until they trigger.\n\nLet me check: was there an order between 15:24 and 17:42 that could have changed the position? I looked at ALL trades and only found those 2. So the position was 191.0 SHORT until 17:42.\n\nThen at 17:42:39, the Algo triggered and bought 202.7. But why 202.7?\n\nMaybe the `closePosition=true` behavior isn't what I think it is. Maybe on Binance testnet, `closePosition=true` on a CONDITIONAL algo order uses a different calculation, or there's a bug.\n\nActually, let me check: `202.7 / 191.0 = 1.061`. That's about 6% more. Could this be related to the leverage or margin?\n\nOr maybe the Algo order was submitted with a specific quantity? Let me re-read the code. `closePosition=true` means \"close the entire position\", but maybe there's ALSO a quantity being set somewhere.\n\nActually, looking at the Algo order creation code again:\n\n```python\nparams = {\n    \"algoType\": \"CONDITIONAL\",\n    \"symbol\": symbol,\n    \"side\": side,\n    \"type\": order_type,\n    \"triggerPrice\": format_price(trigger_price),\n    \"closePosition\": \"true\",\n    \"workingType\": working_type,\n}\n```\n\nThere's no quantity parameter. With `closePosition=true`, Binance should use the current position size. But what if a quantity IS required and Binance uses a default?\n\nActually, for Binance Futures Algo orders with `closePosition=true`, the quantity should be auto-calculated. But maybe on the testnet, this doesn't work correctly and it uses the NOTIONAL value / price as quantity?\n\nNo, that doesn't make sense. 200 / 1.017 = 196.7 ≠ 202.7.\n\nWait: 191.0 * 1.061 = 202.7. The 6% difference could be from something else.\n\nActually, you know what, maybe the simple explanation is: when the SL price was updated to 1.0342, and the current price was around 1.04, the order triggered at a different price than expected. The slippage from the SL trigger to the actual market buy caused the quantity to differ.\n\nNo, that doesn't make sense. A STOP_MARKET algo with closePosition=true would close at the market price, and the quantity should be exactly the position amount.\n\nOK, I think I need to just check: what did the Algo order show? Let me query the Algo history.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_lcWSad9P1EySO6U8p7qQ6533","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace/scripts && python3 -c \"\nfrom binance_auto_trade import request\n# Get Algo order history for DOT\nresp = request('GET', '/fapi/v1/allOrders', {'symbol': 'DOTUSDT', 'limit': 50})\nfrom datetime import datetime\nif resp and isinstance(resp, list):\n    for t in resp:\n        ts = datetime.fromtimestamp(float(t.get('time',0))/1000).strftime('%m-%d %H:%M:%S')\n        if ts.startswith('06-04 15:') or ts.startswith('06-04 16:') or ts.startswith('06-04 17:'):\n            print(f'{ts} {t.get(\\\"side\\\")} qty={t.get(\\\"origQty\\\")} price={t.get(\\\"price\\\")} avg={t.get(\\\"avgPrice\\\")} type={t.get(\\\"type\\\")} reduce={t.get(\\\"reduceOnly\\\")} closePos={t.get(\\\"closePosition\\\")} status={t.get(\\\"status\\\")} id={t.get(\\\"orderId\\\")}')\n\" 2>&1 | grep -v \"InsecureRequestWarning\\|warnings.warn\\|已加载\\|API key\\|🔧\\|✅\"","timeout":15}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":194,"output":2316,"cacheRead":114048,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":116558,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1780574233631}}
