{"type":"message","id":"0e418021","parentId":"a23e7398","timestamp":"2026-06-04T12:03:07.120Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Conversation info (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"message_id\": \"om_x100b6d22db66f4b0b1790f89d5e8d61\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"ou_6d6f52f26015356263395c9b4abfd166\",\n  \"conversation_label\": \"oc_a04a61675c784d76c345fde6501e8f49\",\n  \"sender\": \"用户124241\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Thu 2026-06-04 20:02 GMT+8\",\n  \"group_subject\": \"oc_a04a61675c784d76c345fde6501e8f49\",\n  \"is_group_chat\": true\n}\n```\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"用户124241 (ou_6d6f52f26015356263395c9b4abfd166)\",\n  \"id\": \"ou_6d6f52f26015356263395c9b4abfd166\",\n  \"name\": \"用户124241\"\n}\n```\n\n[message_id: om_x100b6d22db66f4b0b1790f89d5e8d61]\n用户124241: 传精度会不会影响到开仓平仓"}],"timestamp":1780574587103}}
{"type":"message","id":"580fc7ef","parentId":"0e418021","timestamp":"2026-06-04T12:03:38.126Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking whether passing exact quantity (precision) to Algo orders would affect opening/closing positions. This is a good question - they're thinking about the fix I proposed.\n\nLet me think through this carefully:\n\n1. **Algo SL/TP orders** are used for automatic stop-loss and take-profit. They don't directly open or close positions - they're conditional orders that trigger a MARKET order when the price condition is met.\n\n2. If we change from `closePosition=true` to explicit `quantity`, the Algo order will submit a MARKET order with that exact quantity when triggered. The precision of the quantity matters because Binance has specific stepSize requirements.\n\n3. The quantity we pass needs to be formatted to the correct precision for the symbol (e.g., DOT has 1 decimal, LINK has 2 decimals, etc.). This is already handled by `format_quantity()`.\n\n4. For closing positions: if we pass the exact position quantity (e.g., 191.0 DOT), the Algo trigger will close exactly that amount. After closing, the position will be zero - no residual. This is BETTER than `closePosition=true` which has been unreliable.\n\n5. For opening positions: the Algo orders are submitted AFTER the position is opened. The SL/TP orders reference the position size that was just opened. So precision is already handled by the open order.\n\n6. The key risk: if the position quantity changes between when the Algo is submitted and when it triggers (e.g., partial close), the Algo might try to close more than the position size, creating an opposite-direction position. But this risk also exists with `closePosition=true` (and we've seen it happen).\n\n7. Better approach: use `closePosition=true` AND `quantity` together. Or, on Algo trigger, use `closePosition=true` for the actual market close. But we can't control what Binance does when the Algo triggers.\n\nActually, the safest approach is:\n- Keep Algo orders using `closePosition=true` \n- But in `close_position()` function (which we already fixed to use API quantity), close positions using exact quantities via regular MARKET orders\n- Add a safety check: if the Algo triggers and creates a residual, the sync mechanism detects it\n\nWait, but the user's question is about whether passing explicit quantity to Algo would cause issues. Let me think about potential problems:\n\nFor the Algo SL: when submitted with quantity=191.0 and trigger, it buys 191.0 DOT. This closes exactly the 191.0 DOT SHORT. ✓\n\nFor the Algo TP: same logic. ✓\n\nThe risk is if the position is partially closed manually before the Algo triggers. Then the Algo would try to close 191.0 when only 100 are open, potentially creating an 91.0 reverse position. But:\n1. We don't do partial closes\n2. `close_position()` closes the full position\n3. If a partial close happened, `closePosition=true` would have the same issue (it closes \"the position\" which might have changed)\n\nActually with `closePosition=true`, the behavior should be atomic - at the moment of trigger, it looks at the current position and closes it. With explicit quantity, it submits a fixed quantity regardless.\n\nSo the question is: which is safer?\n- `closePosition=true`: adapts to current position, but testnet calculates wrong\n- Explicit quantity: fixed amount, but if position changed, could be wrong\n\nThe BEST solution would be to:\n1. Cancel and resubmit Algo orders whenever the position changes\n2. Use explicit quantity matching the current position\n3. The sync/dynamic SL mechanism already does this!\n\nSo the answer is: yes, transmitting precise quantity is safe because:\n- We already update SL/TP orders dynamically whenever position or SL price changes\n- The quantity is always synced to the current position\n- No partial closes happen in our system\n\nLet me give a concise answer to the user.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"不会。因为 Algo 单只控制止盈止损的触发量，不参与开仓。\n\n关键区别：\n- **开仓**用的是 `place_order()` → 走正常的 MARKET 单，跟我们量化公式算出的 qty，精度已经通过 `format_quantity()` 对齐了，不受 Algo 影响\n- **平仓**触发时，Algo 只是\"代发一笔市价单\"。传精确 qty（比如 191.0 DOT）跟现在 `closePosition=true` 做的是同一件事，区别在于——我们自己算，不再让币安测试网瞎算\n\n你担心的情况是如果仓位中途变了、Algo 还按旧量平会导致甩到对面去。但这个在我们的系统里不会发生，因为 SL 每轮都跟着当前持仓量同步更新（`sync_stop_loss_to_binance`），仓位变了 SL 也跟着重建，不存在\"旧量\"问题。\n\n反而 `closePosition=true` 才是毒瘤——测试网给算出了 202.7，凭空多了 11.7。\n\n要我开改？"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":34095,"output":1895,"cacheRead":18560,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":54550,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1780574587116}}
